<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?><x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="xmp-writer"><rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"><rdf:Description rdf:about="" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"  xmlns:xmp="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/"  xmlns:xmpMM="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/mm/"  xmlns:xmpTPg="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/t/pg/"  xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/" ><dc:title><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Psychometric evaluation of the Penn State Worry Questionnaire: a scoping review protocol</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:title><dc:description><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">The Penn State Worry Questionnaire (PSWQ) is a widely used 16-item measure of trait worry, and its psychometric literature has reached markedly different conclusions about the structure and dimensionality of the instrument. We suspect that much of this divergence reflects how studies interpret model fit, and in particular a reliance on rule-of-thumb cutoffs, together with variation in the choice of estimator for confirmatory factor analysis and in whether and how local independence of the items is evaluated. This protocol describes a scoping review that maps the psychometric literature on the PSWQ published since 2010 and charts not only what each study concluded but how it reached that conclusion. Eligible records are empirical studies that report an original psychometric evaluation of a PSWQ version that evaluates a specified structure, including Rasch measurement theory, item response theory, and confirmatory factor analysis. Any population and setting are included, and eligibility is limited to reports written in English. The search pairs an instrument-only search for sensitivity with an instrument-and-methods search for precision, run across open databases (OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Europe PMC, and PubMed). For each study we chart the method, estimator, and dimensional conclusion, together with the criteria used to interpret model fit, and we record whether and how each of four measurement properties, unidimensionality, local independence, invariance, and ordered response-category thresholds, was evaluated. Findings will be summarized descriptively rather than pooled.</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:description><pdf:Keywords>Penn State Worry Questionnaire, PSWQ, scoping review, psychometrics, Rasch, item response theory, factor analysis</pdf:Keywords><dc:creator><rdf:Seq><rdf:li>Magnus Johansson, Tove Wahlund &amp; Maria Hedman-LagerlÃ¶f</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:creator><xmp:CreatorTool>Typst 0.14.2</xmp:CreatorTool><dc:language><rdf:Bag><rdf:li>en-US</rdf:li></rdf:Bag></dc:language><xmp:ModifyDate>2026-09-24T09:30:37+02:00</xmp:ModifyDate><xmp:CreateDate>2026-09-24T09:30:37+02:00</xmp:CreateDate><xmpTPg:NPages>15</xmpTPg:NPages><dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format><xmpMM:InstanceID>uVM6KXSBWvI3bobDgsMHqQ==</xmpMM:InstanceID><xmpMM:DocumentID>WkZgYPFqfmsvimZXuHTkDg==</xmpMM:DocumentID><xmpMM:RenditionClass>proof</xmpMM:RenditionClass><pdf:PDFVersion>1.7</pdf:PDFVersion></rdf:Description></rdf:RDF></x:xmpmeta><?xpacket end="r"?>
